-- chem/bondpoly.hypercubical — the characteristic polynomial of the opposite-nuclei bond operator, exactly, closing by
-- machine the reduction chem/h2plus-net.hypercubical does by hand. With the nuclei four lines apart (vertices 4, 9) the
-- walled cage's hop and potential are constant on the three classes {4,9}, {0,1,2,3}, {5,6,7,8} (chem/h2plus-net's
-- `hp-sectors` checks the edge counts: a nucleus has 2 lines to the first class, each first-class vertex 1 line back
-- and 2 to the third, each third-class vertex 2 back). The symmetric (Perron) reduction is the 3×3 matrix with
-- diagonal 280·U = (0, 118, 140) above the nuclei and off-diagonal hops a between {4,9} and {0,1,2,3} and b between
-- {0,1,2,3} and {5,6,7,8}, where a² = 2s and b² = 4s, s = (280 t/L)² (the symmetric element across classes of edge
-- counts 2·1 and 2·2). Its characteristic polynomial in x (the level above the nuclei, in 280·E/L) is
--   det(xI − M) = x(x−118)(x−140) − b² x − a²(x−140) = x(x−118)(x−140) − s(6x − 280) = p_f,
-- exactly the cubic chem/h2plus-net uses (`bp-charpoly`, a polynomial identity in x and s). So the opposite placement's
-- lowest level is the least root of p_f, not a bound: the reduction is now checked, not asserted. The sharing
-- placement's p_n is a variational upper bound (a smaller invariant subspace), so the comparison of chem/h2plus-net's
-- `hp-bond-range` stands as: exact opposite vs an upper bound on sharing, which is the right direction for "sharing is
-- lower." A leaf: no module imports it.
import "algebra.hypercubical"

